-- title:
--   Degree [K(j)(j(qᵈ)):K(j)]=ψ(d) over any field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $d$ be a nonzero natural number. Inside the field $K((q))$ of formal Laurent series over $K$, write $j =$ `jqModC K` for the series $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K$ of the integral power series `jNum` $= E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$ (the $q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant with its integer coefficients read in $K$), and for $N \ge 1$ write $j(q^N) =$ `jqNModC K N` for the image of $j$ under the ring endomorphism `qExpand K N` of $K((q))$ that multiplies all exponents by $N$, i.e. substitutes $q^N$ for $q$. Let $K(j)$ denote the intermediate field of $K((q))/K$ generated by $j$. Assume that for every prime $p$ dividing $d$ the series $j(q^p)$ does not lie in $K(j)$. Then the intermediate field of $K((q))/K(j)$ generated by $j(q^d)$ has finite rank over $K(j)$ equal to `dedekindPsi d`, defined as $\sum_{e \mid d,\ e \text{ squarefree}} d/e$, which is Dedekind's $\psi(d) = d\prod_{p \mid d}(1 + 1/p)$.
--
--   This is the classical degree computation for the modular equation, $[K(j(q),j(q^d)) : K(j(q))] = \psi(d)$, here in the purely formal $q$-expansion model and over an arbitrary field, the hypothesis on primes dividing $d$ excluding in particular the characteristic $p$ case where $j(q^p) = j(q)^p$ lies in $K(j)$. It feeds the irreducibility of the modular polynomial $\Phi_N$ over $K(j)$ and the count of poles of $j$ on the function field model of $X_0(N)$.
-- source:
